Directions for Using Graphlan 05/18/26:

Graphlan is not compatible with any version of Python that isn't <3.0 (at present).

So you want to create a new conda environment with the last pre-3.0 version of Python which is 2.7.18. 

However, you can't install that by normal means. The usual conda create, brew install, etc., won't provide access through bioconda/anaconda compatible with ARM (won't work if you have a newer Mac). pyenv apparently somewhat competes with Conda in that they're both trying to do the same thing, so it installs python somewhere that conda doesn't see it (that could be sorted out, but not worth it). This, however, works (if you have a Mac with an M1/M2/etc.,. chip).

#Run in the command line to create a new conda environment with the correct version
CONDA_SUBDIR=osx-64 conda create -n py27 python=2.7.18
conda activate py27
conda config --env --set subdir osx-64


#To install Graphlan, run in command line from within the activated py27 conda environment:

conda install -c bioconda graphlan


#To visualize your taxonomic profiles visually using GraPhlAn, you must first convert your MetaPhlAn output table into GraPhlAn-compatible tree and annotation files using the helper tool


