***********************DIRECTIONS FOR USING HCLUST2 for Heat Cluster Creation:****************

1. Create a new conda environment

conda create -n hclust2
conda activate hclust2

2. Install Hclust2:



3. Combine all of your individual sample output files into a single master abundance table that you wanted displayed. I.e. Merge the metaphlan tables you want to show. If you already have a  merged metaphlan table created from using the biobakery workflows pipeline which produces its own merged file for Metaphlan output, you can use that instead. It should be called something like "merged_abundance_table.tsv" or .txt.


#To merge samples:
merge_metaphlan_tables.py sample1_profile.txt sample2_profile.txt ... > merged_abundance_table.txt


4) If you haven't already, make sure your table headers are relabeled to match your sample id naming conventions. I created a sample_mapping.sed file to do this for you with instructions found in "Sed script for renaming sample Ids in metaphlan abundance table".

5) If you haven't already, process the merged_abundance_table to show only the level of phylogeny that you want to examine (hint: species is going to be far too many to reasonably show almost all of the time). Use the below listed command to get the genus or species listed WITHOUT the taxonomical inheritance (Kingdom, Phyla, etc.,.) before it:

grep -E "s__|ID" merged_abundance_table_relabeled.txt| grep -v "t__" | sed "s/^.*|//g" > merged_abundance_table_species_relabeled.txt

grep -E "g__|clade_name" merged_abundance_table_relabeled.txt | grep -v "s__" | sed -r 's/.*\|g__([^|]+)/\1/' > merged_abundance_table_genus_only_relabeled.txt

#Note if you want to retain the taxonomical inheritance info, you can use the following command  structure instead, (i.e. just drop the sed commands at the end):

grep -E "g__|clade_name" input_table.txt | grep -v "s__" > genus_only_table.txt


6) Run your merged abundance table Metaphlan data through hclust2.

Here is where it gets fun. It should be as easy as running the command below, but it's not.... skip ahead then come back to this.

hclust2.py -i merged_abundance_table_genus_only_relabeled.txt \
  -o metaphlan_heatmap.png \
  --f_dist_f correlation \
  --s_dist_f braycurtis \
  --cell_aspect_ratio 0.5 \
  -l \
  --ftop 50 \
  --fperc 99 \
  --flabel_size 8 \
  --slabel_size 8 \
  --max_flabel_len 100 \
  --max_slabel_len 100 \
