Sample Selection (--sperc + --stop):Similarly, if you have a massive dataset and only want to visualize the samples with the highest (or lowest) values, --sperc sets the specific statistical percentile used to rank the samples before keeping the top --stop samples.

Feature Filtering (--fperc + --ftop):When generating a heatmap, you rarely want to plot all hundreds of bacterial clades detected. If you set --ftop 50 --fperc 99, hclust2 calculates the 99th percentile of abundance for every species across all samples. It then ranks those species by that number and isolates the top \(50\) species.Example: Changing --fperc (e.g., from 99 to 100 [maximum abundance]) will change which species make the top --ftop list because you are measuring a different metric of their distribution.


To create a heatmap of top differential pathways, use HUMAnN to profile pathways from your metagenomic sequences. Then, perform differential abundance testing (using tools like MaAsLin2 or lefse) to find significant pathways, format the resulting table into a matrix, and use hclust2 or an R package (like pheatmap) for plotting.


